seoDescription: >-
  A cofounder equity split is the talk most Indian founders delay. Here is how
  to open it, what to agree on, and how to put it in writing before trouble
  starts.
---
Aarav and Kabir shook hands on a Saturday evening in a Koramangala café, with two filter coffees going cold between them. The 90-day trial had ended well. Loopwise AI had its first paying pilot, and the two old college roommates were now officially building it together. Kabir raised his cup and said, "Equity we'll sort out later. Let's first get customers."

Aarav agreed, because it felt like the generous thing to say. Four months later, an investor asked a simple question: "Who owns what?" Neither of them had a clear answer. This is the story of a **cofounder equity split** that nearly broke a friendship before the company had a single lakh in revenue. It is Part 2 of the Loopwise AI story. If you are new here, [Part 1 covers how Aarav chose his cofounder](https://www.simpleworks.in/blog/how-to-choose-a-cofounder-for-your-startup). Aarav and Kabir are fictional. The problem is not. My view is that equity is not a number you calculate once. It is the first big decision two founders make together, and delaying it costs far more than the discomfort of having it.

---

## Where Aarav and Kabir stood

Before the split makes sense, you need the facts behind it.

Aarav had spent fourteen months building the Loopwise product alone, mostly at night while holding a day job. He put in about ₹18 lakh of his own savings, on servers, tools and a part-time designer. Kabir had spent those same months in enterprise sales at a software firm. When the trial ended, he quit his job to join Loopwise full-time, giving up a stable salary.

Both men now treated the company as theirs. Neither had asked what "theirs" meant in shares.

A few terms matter here, so let me define them plainly. **Equity** is the ownership of the company, held as shares. **Vesting** means you earn your shares over time instead of receiving them all on day one. A **cliff** is a period, often twelve months, before which nothing vests. If a founder leaves before the cliff, they leave with no shares. These three ideas do most of the work in a good agreement.

Aarav and Kabir had none of them. They had a handshake and an unspoken assumption, and the assumption was different in each man's head. Aarav privately felt that fourteen months of work and ₹18 lakh deserved a clear lead. Kabir privately felt that two people who both quit everything to join should split things down the middle. Neither said so aloud, because they were friends.

---

## Why 50-50 feels fair and often fails

When the investor's question finally pushed them into the conversation, Kabir proposed the obvious answer: fifty-fifty. Aarav hesitated, then agreed, because arguing felt worse than conceding.

They took the problem to Arjun Rao, the advisor from Part 1. He listened, then asked, "Who breaks the tie?"

Neither man had thought about it. That is the first weakness of an equal split. With two founders at 50% each, a disagreement about hiring, pricing or fundraising has no resolution. Both can block, and neither can decide. A company in its first year decides dozens of things a week, so a built-in stalemate is expensive.

The second weakness is subtler. An equal split treats the past and the future as identical for both people. But Aarav had already put in fourteen months and ₹18 lakh. Kabir was putting in his next four years. Both contributions are real, and an equal split ignores the difference between them.

There is research behind this unease. Noam Wasserman, who studied founding teams for his book *The Founder's Dilemmas* (2012), argues that many teams split equity equally and quickly, and that a fast equal split often avoids a hard conversation instead of settling it. The point is not that equal splits are always wrong. The point is that speed often signals avoidance.

I have seen the same pattern in Indian founder teams. Two friends split things down the middle to protect the friendship, and then the unspoken resentment arrives later, when the company is worth something and the stakes are high.

---

## Four questions that set a fair cofounder equity split

Arjun did not hand Aarav and Kabir a formula. He gave them four questions and asked them to answer separately, on paper, before comparing. This matters because founders who answer aloud tend to agree with whoever speaks first.

**1. What has each person already contributed?** This covers the idea, the product, the money and the relationships built before the company existed. Aarav's fourteen months and ₹18 lakh counted here. So did Kabir's introduction that led to the first pilot.

**2. What risk does each person carry from today?** Quitting a job, putting in personal savings and signing personal guarantees are all risk. Kabir had given up a salary. Aarav had given up his savings and his evenings for over a year. Both were exposed, though in different ways.

**3. How committed is each person, and for how long?** Full-time versus part-time matters a great deal. Both men were now full-time, which pushed the split closer together.

**4. How hard is each person to replace, and what will they do over the next four years?** Aarav's role was product and engineering. Kabir's role was revenue. In a B2B software company, strong sales leadership is as scarce as strong engineering. Neither skill was easier to hire than the other.

When they compared answers, the gap was smaller than either feared, and it ran in a direction neither had said aloud. Aarav's past contribution justified a lead. Kabir's future contribution justified nearly matching it. They settled on **55% for Aarav and 45% for Kabir**, with any future employee option pool to dilute both of them equally. This was not a calculation. It was a negotiated answer to four honest questions, and both men could explain why the numbers were what they were.

---

## Vesting for cofounders: so leaving costs something

The percentage was only half the work. Arjun's next question was harder: "What happens if one of you leaves in eighteen months?"

Without vesting, the answer is brutal. A founder who walks away takes their whole stake with them, and the person who stays has to build the company while a departed cofounder owns nearly half of it. Investors see this as a serious red flag. Many will not fund a company where founders have fully owned shares from day one.

Aarav and Kabir agreed on a **four-year vesting schedule with a one-year cliff**. Shares vest monthly after the first year, so after four years, each man owns his full stake. If either leaves in the first year, he leaves with nothing. If he leaves in year two, he keeps what has vested and the rest returns to the company.

Aarav also negotiated one adjustment, and it is a good example of fairness over formula. Because he had already worked for fourteen months, a quarter of his shares were treated as vested on day one, as credit for work done. The remaining three-quarters vested over the four years. Kabir agreed immediately, because the logic was visible.

In India, this is usually written into a **shareholders' agreement**, which sits alongside the company's articles under the Companies Act, 2013. Vesting is typically enforced by giving the company or the remaining founders a right to buy back unvested shares at a nominal price if a founder leaves. The details depend on your company structure, so have a lawyer draft it. Do not copy a template from a blog, including this one.

They also wrote down decision rights. Aarav had the final say on product. Kabir had the final say on sales and customer commitments. For shared matters like fundraising and hiring senior roles, Arjun was named as the agreed tie-breaker, with both founders consenting. This one clause removed the stalemate that a 50-50 split had built in.

---

## The objection: "Calculating contributions insults a friend"

When Aarav told another founder friend about all this, the friend pushed back hard. "You are overcomplicating it," he said. "If you trust each other, split it equally and move on. Putting a number on contribution turns a friendship into a negotiation."

This is the strongest objection, and it deserves a fair answer. Trust is the real foundation of any founding team. An agreement cannot rescue a partnership between people who don't trust each other, and a negotiation conducted in a hostile spirit can do harm.

But the objection treats the agreement as a statement of distrust, and that is where it goes wrong. In Aarav's case, the conversation did the opposite. Neither man had known how the other felt. Writing answers on paper separately brought out Aarav's quiet sense of unfairness and Kabir's quiet fear of being treated as junior. Both were resolved in an afternoon. Both would have grown into resentment otherwise.

The friend is right on one point: the process has to be done kindly, with both founders treating it as something they are doing together, not something one does to the other. Where the objection fails is on the claim that avoiding the conversation protects the friendship. In my experience, it postpones the damage to a point where the company is worth fighting over.

---

## What to do this month

If you are a cofounder with no written agreement, or an agreement that says "equal, for now", here is where to start.

Write down your answers to the four questions, separately from your cofounder, before you speak. Compare them in a calm setting. Agree on a vesting schedule, and on who decides when you disagree. Then take it to a lawyer who works with startups and have it written into a shareholders' agreement. If you want help structuring the conversation, [Simpleworks works with founders](https://simpleworks.in/about) on exactly this kind of early decision.

Loopwise AI signed its agreement six weeks after the Koramangala café. The investor's question never came up again.

Equity is the first management decision two founders make together. How they handle it shows each of them how the other will handle every hard decision that follows. The conversation you are avoiding is practice for the larger ones ahead. For more founder-focused writing, see the [Simpleworks blog](https://simpleworks.in/blog).

